  "subtitle": "A professional absolute-positioning graphics tablet with a four-button puck, built on Håkan Lans's phase-shift inductive sensing, that became a standard scientific and drafting instrument",
  "why_scout_cares": "The museum has consumer drawing pads and the Summagraphics Bit Pad, but the HIPAD is a different, weirder animal: a professional digitizer whose sensing was invented by Swedish engineer Håkan Lans as a way to trace seismic measurements, sold across the Atlantic to Houston Instruments, and picked up by DEC, cartographers, seismic crews, and CAD shops. It is a 'pointing device' that is not a mouse and not a stylus — a large tablet with a puck bristling with named buttons (RESET, POINT, SWITCH STREAM, STREAM) that stream coordinates to a host. Its absolute, inductive sensing meant you could lift the puck, lay down a map or seismic record, and pick up a point by locating it with crosshairs. That coupling of a physical document, a crosshair, and a stream of numbers is an interface story worth preserving: it treats the desk itself as the input surface.",
